window.SOURCE = {"title":"FROM RIVERBED TO LIVELIHOOD: SAND AND GRAVEL MINING, RIVER RESPONSE AND THE PERSISTENCE OF SEDIMENT EXTRACTION IN NORTHERN BANGLADESH","author":"Md Sadiul Alam Chyon","file":"Thesis-Draft_Sadiul.docx","draftDate":"2026-11-16","excerpts":{"331":"The overall aim of this thesis is to quantify the extent, physical impacts and social consequences of river sand and gravel mining in northern Bangladesh, and to explain why extraction persists despite recognized harm, in order to inform management that links extraction to the sediment budgets of rivers.","333":"RQ1 (Drivers and extent; Chapter 4). How has the spatial extent of sand and gravel mining in northern Bangladesh changed since the late 1980s, and how is this change related to national economic growth and policy?","335":"RQ2 (Extent, impacts and management; Chapter 5). How does the rate of gravel extraction on the Lubha River compare with its natural sediment transport rate, how do the channel and floodplain respond to extraction and to its cessation, and how does the ban on mining affect the communities that depended on it?","337":"RQ3 (Extent and impacts; Chapters 6 and 7). Has mechanized sand extraction on the Someshwari River altered the channel bed, river stage, the adjacent aquifer and the floodplain, can any such changes be attributed to mining where extraction volumes are unrecorded, and do the Jadukata, Piyain, Sari-Gowain and Lubha rivers show the same changes?","339":"RQ4 (Impacts and management; Chapter 8). To what extent do households along the Someshwari River recognize the harms of sand extraction, and why does extraction persist?","465":"The exposed area of bars and pits therefore depends on the water level when an image is acquired. A bar may shrink in area because water is higher or because the bar surface has been lowered (Figure 3.3c, d), and a pit may appear to grow because it was excavated or because the river rose and inundated more sediment. Every analysis of extent thus uses dry-season images, when bars are exposed and extraction takes place (Section 2.2), and accounts for the possible influence of water stage in one of two ways. Where dry-season water level varies little between years, images are selected at similar stage (Figure 3.3e). Chapter 5 retains only images whose stage at the nearest gauge on the image date falls within a narrow band, and Chapter 4, where most rivers lack suitable gauge records, uses one scene per dry season near the January low-flow minimum. Where river stage varies more, each image is paired with the water level on the same day, and exposed area is analyzed as a function of stage against a relation fitted to images from before extraction (Figure 3.3f; Chapters 6 and 7). A departure from that relation records reduced exposure at a given stage, not its cause or the volume of sediment removed.","470":"The channel bed is surveyed directly only at repeat cross-sections, on the Someshwari River and on three of the four rivers of Chapter 7, and only in some years. Elsewhere, and between surveys, bed change is inferred from the relation between stage and discharge. If the bed is lowered, the water level is lower at the same discharge. At low flow, stage is usually set by the bed topography acting as a section control rather than by the whole channel (WMO, 2010), and thus lowering of the bed should lower water stage most at low flow. A lower stage at the same discharge shows only that the relation has changed, which can also result from a change in roughness or in a downstream control (Rantz et al., 1982). The comparison is also valid only where discharge is measured independently of stage, because a discharge computed from the gauge’s own rating curve would reproduce the old relation. The analyses therefore use only measured discharges, which BWDB obtains with current meters and, at some stations, acoustic Doppler current profilers (Section 3.3.2). The discharges supplied for all gauges are dated values at intervals of two weeks to a month, not daily series, as expected of field measurements rather than of values computed from a rating.","475":"Two limits shape how the results of this sequence are reported. First, some patterns can fit more than one explanation. A fall that is uniform across flows fits lowering transmitted along the reach as well as excavation at the gauge. Such a component is located by comparing gauges and, where available, cross-sections. Second, gauge records cannot separate lowering caused by extraction in the gauged reach from lowering caused by reduced sediment supply from upstream, including supply reduced by extraction farther upstream. Separating these requires a dated record of extraction, which the onset dates of Chapter 4 provide for the rivers of Chapters 6 and 7 within Bangladesh. Upstream of the border, the January images of Chapter 4 show no persistent change, but extraction there is not otherwise recorded (Section 3.8). Results are therefore reported as the explanations that remain after each test. Because the sequence relies only on records that routine monitoring already produces, it can be applied to other rivers where extraction is unrecorded.","478":"These methods share two limits. First, serial correlation within a record makes a trend appear more significant than it is (Hirsch et al., 1982). Chapter 7 therefore resamples whole years to obtain its confidence intervals, whereas the annual series of Chapter 6 were tested without adjustment, and their significance may be overstated where successive years are correlated. Second, a change point locates when a record shifted, and not why, and thus its date is compared with the independently dated phases of extraction, allowing different variables and locations to respond at different times.","480":"Neither social survey could draw a probability sample, because no household list or complete sampling frame exists for the study communities. On the Lubha River, respondents were selected purposively to include the main livelihood groups of a small community in which key actors are known to residents (Etikan et al., 2016). On the Someshwari River, accessible adults were recruited by convenience near the riverside sand landings, which concentrated the sample among households closest to extraction and among men (Figure 3.5). Such samples support comparisons between groups within a sample, not estimates of prevalence in the wider population (Cornesse et al., 2020), and their statistical tests describe associations within the sample, not causes. Patterns are therefore reported across occupational or livelihood groups, and frequencies are not extrapolated beyond the respondents.","487":"Two methods are each specific to one chapter but central to a hypothesis, and thus their rationale is explained herein. Chapter 4 dates extraction at fixed sites rather than mapping it across the whole region. A random-forest classification of the border corridor was tested first and set aside, because it detected excavated ground only where extraction left a large, persistent bare surface (Section 4.2.1; Appendix 4A.4). Measuring the areas of water and sand inside a fixed outline, in one January scene per dry season, applies one index rule across four sensors and 38 seasons without training data, and the onset dates on which Hypothesis 1 depends are taken from persistent change in those areas. The class of each site is checked against sub-meter imagery rather than a stratified accuracy sample, and the onset dates are interpreted as the latest likely dates of extraction (Section 4.2.4). Chapter 8 builds a qualitative causal loop diagram, a method often criticized for links that are not tied to evidence (Richardson, 1986). Chapter 8 therefore records the polarity of each link, its survey, interview and documentary evidence, and whether it is supported by triangulated evidence or retained as an inference, which allows Hypothesis 4 to be tested rather than illustrated.","490":"Because extraction at the study sites involves politically connected operators and, on the Someshwari River, was under legal challenge during fieldwork (Section 2.5), politically sensitive material is reported only by de-identified role, and no individual is named in connection with specific statements. The household survey file contains coded identifiers but no names, interview files that retain identifying details are stored separately from analysis outputs, and transcripts and field forms are not released. Permission was obtained for the field photographs in which people appear.","501":"The first set of limitations concerns extraction itself. Volumes are recorded at no study site. On the Lubha River they are estimated from pit areas and assigned depths, and elsewhere extraction is inferred from imagery and its physical consequences. Outside the Lubha and Someshwari rivers, whose chronologies also draw on fieldwork (Chapters 5 and 6), the timing of extraction relies on the January Landsat record of Chapter 4. Detection at 30 m lags the start of extraction where pits are small, and dredging in a channel already full of water at low flow leaves no January signal. The onset dates are therefore the latest likely dates, and some extraction is not detected (Section 4.2.4). The drivers of extraction are represented by national series, and thus Hypothesis 1 compares the timing of extraction at many sites with indicators that vary only from year to year at the national scale. Finally, the catchments and most sediment sources of the study rivers lie in India (Section 2.1.3), and without records of supply or of extraction upstream of the border, changes in sediment supply cannot be assessed directly.","502":"The second set of limitations concerns the physical records. Imagery is limited by spatial resolution, which can miss small artisanal pits, by cloud cover and scan-line gaps, and by differences in water stage between image dates (Section 3.4.1). The radar flood maps of Chapter 6 were validated against water mapped from Sentinel-2, but the maps of pits (Chapter 5), site classes (Chapter 4) and exposed bars (Chapters 6 and 7) were checked against field observation or other imagery, and not by a formal accuracy assessment with independent reference samples. The monitoring records contain gaps and changes in practice, discharge is measured only intermittently, and survey methods changed over the records. Cross-sections were resurveyed in only two to five years on three of the rivers of Chapter 7 and never on the Lubha River, and thus bed lowering between surveys, and at every date on the Lubha River, is inferred from one or two gauges per river rather than measured. Resampled confidence intervals describe uncertainty given the observations retained, not systematic errors in the records.","503":"The third set of limitations concerns the social evidence. Both surveys used non-probability samples, and the Someshwari River sample is concentrated near extraction and almost entirely male; the separate women’s focus groups add perspectives without correcting its composition. Questions concerning earlier conditions rely on recall, and the records of the first Someshwari River focus groups are incomplete. The social research was also conducted in January and February 2024, while extraction on the Someshwari River was under legal challenge and before the change of government in August 2024, and thus responses reflect conditions at that time.","542":"The dates in this chapter are subject to three limits, each of which further data could reduce. First, detection with 30 m imagery tends to lag the start of extraction where pits are small. The January time series of the Lubha River changes in 2013, a year after pits were first mapped (Chapter 5), and that of the Someshwari River changes in 2021, c. nine years after extraction began and four years after it was mechanized (Chapter 6). The onset dates of extraction are therefore best interpreted as the latest likely dates, and higher-resolution imagery would bring them closer to the actual start of extraction. Such imagery is available only from the late 2010s or for single dates, and thus it cannot yet give a consistent record back to 1988. Second, the change from Landsat 7 to Landsat 8 in 2013 affects the classes on bare bars, because the Landsat 8 sensor has narrower spectral bands and a different calibration. In pairs of scenes taken a few days apart in the same season, Landsat 8 gives NDVI values c. 0.06 higher, which results in 17% less sand and 1.6 times as much vegetation (Appendix 4A.5). When the earlier spectral bands are transformed to match Landsat 8 (Roy et al., 2016), 32 of 36 declines in bar vegetation and all 19 breaks in water area at the dredging sites remain significant. However, onset dates in 2013 and 2014 have an uncertainty of c. two years. Third, river stage is controlled at most sites only by the choice of a January scene. Where gauge records exist, the stage-at-discharge test of Section 3.4.2 adds a check (Chapters 6 and 7), and more gauge records would extend this test to further rivers. Two forms of extraction are also difficult to detect with the present design. The first is the lowering of a wide bed as a whole, as on the Someshwari River upstream of Durgapur, which leaves the areas of water and sand unchanged. The second is extraction in channels narrower than 30 m, which is recorded in the lease lists and the land-use map (Section 4.3.5). Repeat bed surveys and sub-meter imagery would capture both more fully, but repeat bed surveys exist only for the Someshwari River and three of the rivers of Chapter 7, at a few sections each.","545":"Of the 337 sites, 179 show a change in the area of water, sand or bare ground that persists through successive January scenes. Each of these sites was assigned a class and, where the change has a clear start, the first year of extraction was dated (Figures 4.3 and 4.4). The other 158 sites show no persistent change. Most of them are reported extraction sites where extraction was too short-lived or too small to be detected in the January Landsat time series, and the rest are control reaches and screen cells (Section 4.2.3). These 158 sites are kept in the site set, but they are neither dated nor measured. Seventy-nine of the 179 sites are identified as an extraction type. They comprise 14 sites of pit extraction, 16 fields of floodplain pits, 34 reaches of possible dredging and 15 reaches of bar loss. Twenty-eight sites record the storage or use of extracted material, as 15 depots and stockpiles and 13 blocks of filled land. The remaining 72 sites record bar vegetation, the growth of vegetation on the chars of the Teesta, Dharla and Brahmaputra rivers. Bar vegetation is not extraction, and it extends upstream into India as far as the Sevoke Gorge, where the Teesta River leaves the Himalayan foothills (Figure 4.3a; Section 4.3.3).","598":"The record of extraction established in this chapter supports the governance part of Hypothesis 1 and is consistent with its demand part in timing. The agreement with demand is closer for the growth of the footprint than for the onset of new sites, and the record does not support the rival explanation of local control. On the demand side, the site record is compared with national cement production, the closest annual index of demand for concrete (Andrew, 2019; Figure 4.13a, d). Cement output in Bangladesh rose from 0.3 Mt in 1995 to 5 Mt in 2005, 14 Mt in 2010 and 32 to 34 Mt from 2019. Seventy-two of the 77 dated onsets of extraction occur from 2000 onward, and 37 of them in 2000 to 2009, the decade in which cement output grew more than tenfold (Figure 4.13b). The footprint of extraction grows from c. 260 ha in 2000 to 540 ha in 2010 and 880 ha in 2020 (Figure 4.6a). The footprint and cement output share one monotonic trend (Spearman rho = 0.97 for 1990 to 2022), but their five-year changes are only weakly related (rho = 0.32). The record is thus consistent with demand as the driver of extraction, but it does not measure that demand. After 2014, the two series diverge. Cement output doubles, the onset of new sites nearly stops (66 sites by 2014 and 77 by 2023), and the footprint grows from c. 570 ha in 2014 to c. 1,000 ha in 2023. The growth of the last decade thus likely came from more intensive extraction at existing sites and from the later extraction on the sand-bed rivers, rather than from new extraction sites. National cement output, like GDP, indexes the growth of construction across Bangladesh as a whole. It shows when the demand for sand and gravel grew, but not where the sand and gravel mined in the study area were used (Section 4.1).","599":"The governance part of Hypothesis 1 is supported by the timing of the interruptions to extraction and by the displacement of extraction, that is, its movement to other places and materials after a ban. The interruptions coincide with governance events, such as bans, lease cancellations and changes of government, rather than with hydrological events such as floods or droughts. Gravel extraction at Jaflong and Bholaganj stopped in 2020. Under the national ban of 18 February 2020, extraction was suspended at all gravel extraction sites in Bangladesh (The Daily Star, 2025), and the leases of the Sylhet extraction sites were canceled later that year. On the Lubha River, extraction stopped in February 2021, when the ban there took effect (Section 2.5). Extraction resumed at Jaflong and Bholaganj after August 2024 (Chowdhury, 2025). The Patgram pit fields shrank after their closure in 2016. On the Someshwari River, the High Court stay of March 2024 interrupted leasing but not extraction (Section 2.5.1; Chapter 8). However, the bans did not reduce the regional extent of extraction. The footprint increased from c. 640 ha in 2015 to c. 1,000 ha in 2023 (Figure 4.6a), and seven of the 13 sites legible on the yearly PlanetScope mosaics were still active in 2025 to 2026 (Table 4.5). The displacement of gravel extraction is visible in the trade record. Imports of pebbles, gravel and crushed stone from India and Bhutan increased from c. 0.5 Mt year⁻¹ in 2014 and 2015 to 2.9 Mt year⁻¹ in 2018 and 7.5 Mt year⁻¹ in 2020, the year the Sylhet leases were canceled, and stayed at 6 to 7 Mt year⁻¹ to 2023 (United Nations, 2026; Figure 4.13c). The two areas of gravel extraction, in S1 and S6, were mined from the late 1990s and reached their largest number of sites by 2007 and 2013, respectively (Figure 4.5b). The sand-bed rivers of Sunamganj and Netrakona were the last to change. Together with the dredged Old Brahmaputra River, they were the only parts of the study area in Bangladesh where the number of extraction sites was still increasing after 2015. The bans thus changed the location and the material of extraction in three ways: i) from gravel at the leased sites to sand on rivers outside the regulated zone, ii) to imported stone at the former gravel extraction sites, and iii) to dredged sand sold from spoil yards. This is the response that Hypothesis 1 predicts. A similar displacement followed the ban on sand mining in the Yangtze River, after which dredging moved into Poyang Lake from 2001 (de Leeuw et al., 2010).","602":"The Dhaka series indicates the scale of demand for sand, not the destination of the sand mined in the study area. At the mean fill thickness of 3.05 m measured in the field, the 72 km² filled east of Dhaka by 2020 represents c. 350 million tonnes of sand placed in nineteen years. Combined with per-capita concrete use and the import value of crushed stone, this gave a first national estimate for 2020 of at least 18 Mt of sand (8 Mt as fill and 10 Mt in concrete) and 15 Mt of gravel, of which 14 Mt was used in concrete and most was imported after the Sylhet extraction sites closed (Islam, 2023). These are lower bounds from one year and one city. Applying the ratios of aggregate to cement that Zhuang et al. (2025) use for South Asia to the 32 Mt of cement produced in 2020 gives c. 70 Mt of sand and c. 125 Mt of gravel in concrete alone. However, crushed brick replaces much of the gravel in Bangladeshi concrete (Mohammed et al., 2023), and thus the cement-based figures are an upper reference, and cement serves here as an index of demand rather than as a tonnage. The Dhaka fill increases most steeply in 2006 to 2016 (Figure 4.8b), the years in which the gravel-bed rivers of Sylhet filled with extraction sites and the sand-bed rivers of Sunamganj began to change from sand to water (Figures 4.5b and 4.6a). Much of the sand placed in Dhaka, however, came from the Meghna and Padma rivers, which are closer to the city. The match in timing therefore shows that extraction in the north and fill in Dhaka responded to the same growth of demand, which follows national economic growth, and not that sand moved from the northern rivers to Dhaka. A budget linking sources and sinks would need volumes, which Chapter 5 derives at one site and which areas alone cannot provide.","604":"Whether the onset of extraction on the sand-bed rivers was displacement from the gravel-bed rivers or independent growth cannot be determined from the January time series alone. Two interpretations are possible. The first is displacement. The 2014 court order against mechanical extraction at the Sylhet extraction sites and the 2015 declaration of Jaflong as an ecologically critical area (Deshwara, 2017) raised the cost of gravel from Sylhet, and extraction then moved west to sand-bed rivers outside the regulated zone. The conversion of the bed of the Dhopajan-Chalti River at Dolura to water within two seasons of the order is the clearest case. The second interpretation is the independent growth of sand extraction from boats on the Surma River and its tributaries, as demand for sand rose with construction across Bangladesh, the building of roads across the haors and the sand fills inside the study area. Ten of these fills date from 2014 or later (Section 4.3.4). The fills are closer to the mined rivers than Dhaka and are more likely destinations of their sand, but the volumes placed in them are not recorded in the sources used here. The lease record gives a partial test of the two interpretations. The Dhopajan balumahal is listed in the gazette as a sand and gravel extraction site, and its leasing was suspended in 2018, after the conversion of sand to water was complete; the Sharighat and Rakti reaches were leased throughout. At these sites, extraction therefore grew under a lease, rather than in response to a ban elsewhere. A compilation of the lease years and tender values of the Sunamganj balumahals from 2012 to 2018 would separate the two interpretations. In either case, the comparison with national indicators is weaker than the comparison with governance events. GDP, the production of construction minerals and the Dhaka fills all increase monotonically, and thus any growing regional series agrees with them in trend. The information lies instead in the timing of onsets relative to governance events and in the movement of extraction between rivers, both of which the January time series resolves to one or two seasons.","606":"The record of extraction in this chapter measures extraction where sand is converted to standing water at low flow inside a fixed outline. It achieves this on sand-bed rivers, where a classification of dry-season composites detects no extraction (Section 4.2.1). Three types of extraction are not captured by this record, and each is documented by another source. First, artisanal pits smaller than a pixel are detected only once they merge; thus, the January time series of the Lubha River changes in 2013, although pits were mapped from 2012 (Chapter 5), and that of the Someshwari River changes in 2021, c. nine years after extraction began and four years after it was mechanized (Chapter 6). Second, extraction that lowers a wide bed as a whole, or removes boulders from a bed that holds no water in January, leaves no change in area. Examples are the Someshwari River upstream of Durgapur and the Dooars rivers. Third, the narrow streams of Sherpur and Nilphamari, where the land-use map records mining and the 2026 imagery shows re-excavated channels, are narrower than a Landsat pixel. The screens of Section 4.2.3 show the converse. Searching the whole study area at 10 and 30 m resolution, and viewing every remaining reach at least 30 m wide on 0.5 m imagery, added few sites and none in Bangladesh, and thus the site set is complete for rivers of that width. The count of 79 extraction-type sites and the footprint of c. 930 ha are therefore lower bounds on the places mined and the ground altered, and the onsets are the latest likely dates for the start of extraction. However, the pattern of two areas of gravel extraction followed by later extraction on the sand-bed rivers is unlikely to be affected by these limits, because the forms of extraction that are not detected are spread across the study area. The footprint is also not a volume. Most of the sand extracted from a sand-bed river is replaced by sediment deposited in the next monsoon, and thus it leaves no trace in January. The footprint therefore records only the part of extraction that left lasting standing water.","613":"Sand and gravel extraction along Bangladesh’s northern fringe is concentrated in a belt at the foot of the Himalayan foothills and the Shillong Plateau, where rivers deposit their coarse load. Of the 337 sites examined from 1988 to 2025, 79 show a persistent change of an extraction type, and a further 28 are depots and sand fills that serve extraction. The ground altered by extraction grew from c. 260 ha in 2000 to between 870 and 1,000 ha from 2020. More than 50% of this area lies in Sunamganj and Sylhet, and 91% lies in Bangladesh. This footprint is a lower bound, and it is an area, not a volume.","614":"Extraction spread along the border in sequence. It began in two areas of gravel extraction, on the floodplains of Tetulia and Patgram in the west from 1998 and on the gravel-bed rivers of Sylhet in the east from 2000. Extraction sites had spread across the Sylhet rivers by 2013. After 2014, new extraction shifted to the sand-bed rivers of Sunamganj and Netrakona, and after 2019 it also grew as dredging on the Old Brahmaputra River. Crusher and stockpile yards for gravel appeared in 1999 to 2006, sand fills inside the study area mainly in 2014 to 2024, and spoil yards of dredged sand from 2020. These fills are more likely destinations of the sand mined in the study area than Dhaka.","615":"The bans of 2014 to 2021 stopped gravel extraction at the leased Sylhet sites, but they did not reduce the regional extent of extraction. Extraction moved to sand-bed rivers outside the regulated zone, imported stone replaced gravel at the former extraction sites, and dredged sand was sold from spoil yards. Extraction then resumed at the banned sites after August 2024. These results support the governance part of Hypothesis 1, because interruptions to extraction followed governance events rather than floods or droughts, and the bans shifted extraction rather than reducing it.","616":"The record is consistent with the demand part of Hypothesis 1 in timing only. National cement output and the Dhaka sand fill show when demand grew, not where the sand mined in the study area was used. The rival explanation of local control does not account for interruptions that were synchronous across rivers under different lease regimes. Whether the later extraction on the sand-bed rivers was displaced from Sylhet or grew independently cannot yet be determined.","673":"From 2021 to 2024, after the ban took effect in February 2021, the landscape began to recover. With extraction halted, sediment supplied during the monsoon infilled the channel pits permanently (Figures 5.5f, 5.6d and 5.7). By 2024, most channel pits had been filled, leaving only 5.5% of the previously mined area unfilled, and the infill had largely restored the natural morphology of the channel (Figures 5.5f and 5.10). The 2024 image shows reformed alternate bars, active gravel dunes and infilled pits in the active channel. Recovery in the abandoned channels/floodplain is also evident, with agriculture returning near the riverbanks (Figures 5.5f, 5.10 and 5.11). The morphology of the channel in 2024 closely resembled pre-extraction conditions, and pits became smaller and more uniform, with fewer outliers (Figures 5.8 and 5.9).","681":"The infilling of pits between pre-monsoon and post-monsoon images gives an estimate of sediment transport rates (Figure 5.4; Table 5.1). During 2017 to 2021, monsoon infill in the active channel deposited 1.84 Mt of gravel, an average transport rate of 0.37 Mt year⁻¹. This is slightly higher than the 0.30 Mt year⁻¹ estimated by Daham et al. (2024) with the WBMsed model (Cohen et al., 2022) and offers a first-order guide to geomorphologically sustainable extraction. Recovery in the abandoned channels/floodplain has been slower (Figures 5.7 and 5.8), because little floodwater reaches these topographically higher areas: by 2024, only 23% of their 2021 peak pit area had been infilled. Because the imagery cannot quantify partial infilling, this is a minimum. Large, deep pits nonetheless persist (Figures 5.7 and 5.9), which raises the question of how these areas can best be used and what their long-term effects on the landscape will be.","682":"Daham et al. (2024) estimated gravel extraction at Lubhachorra at 0.5 ± 0.1 Mt year⁻¹ for 2016 to 2021, an estimate that may not distinguish the active channel from the abandoned channels/floodplain. For 2016 to 2021, the estimate of total extraction made here (4.94 Mt, or 0.82 Mt year⁻¹) is almost twice their 2.5 Mt. The difference arises because Daham et al. (2024) probably underestimated extraction from large pits by assuming a depth of 3 m rather than the 5 m used here: with a depth of 3 m for all pits, the estimate for 2016 to 2021 becomes 2.39 Mt, close to theirs, which shows the need for field observation and ground-truthing. Over 2012 to 2021, total extraction is estimated at 7.86 Mt, or 0.79 Mt year⁻¹ (4.43 Mt from the active channel and 3.43 Mt from the abandoned channels/floodplain, or 0.44 and 0.34 Mt year⁻¹), closer to their rate.","683":"The average rate of total extraction (c. 0.79 Mt year⁻¹) exceeded the average total transport rate of 0.58 Mt year⁻¹, which is taken here as the geomorphologically sustainable rate of extraction, and extraction peaked in 2020 to 2021 (Figure 5.7). In the active channel, extraction averaged 0.44 Mt year⁻¹ against an infilling rate of 0.37 Mt year⁻¹, exceeding supply by c. 19%. In the abandoned channels/floodplain, extraction averaged 0.34 Mt year⁻¹ against infilling of 0.21 Mt year⁻¹, exceeding it by 61%. Extraction in the active channel was therefore close to the yearly supply of gravel from upstream, whereas the abandoned channels/floodplain received less supply and recovered more slowly. The active channel thus recovered substantially within four years of the ban through sedimentation and the infilling of pits (Figure 5.11c), whereas the abandoned channels/floodplain remain more affected (Figure 5.11a) and will need more time, and targeted restoration, to recover fully.","687":"During peak extraction, temporary laborers outnumbered local residents (Figures 5.12 and 5.13). In 2013, c. 4,800 workers, four times the local population of 2011, came to mine the active channel; in 2020, c. 7,300 workers, again four times the local population of 2021 (Bangladesh Bureau of Statistics, 2013, 2024), worked in both the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain. This reliance on transient labor in remote Lubhachorra contrasts with sand-mining areas elsewhere in Bangladesh, such as Jamalpur, where in-migration was minimal (Hatlebakk et al., 2025).","694":"At first, mining provided financial stability, with workers earning c. 600 BDT (c. 6 USD) a day. A former miner, aged 45, recalled, “People could easily survive with the money from our daily work.” The ban ended this income abruptly: 88% of respondents said it brought economic decline, and 95% that it led to unemployment (Table 5.2). Many former miners and farmers turned to illegal activities such as sugar smuggling. A former miner, aged 37, said, “People are now forced to risk their lives… One individual was killed in an Indian Border Security Force firing a few days ago.” Debt became a further burden, because many had borrowed to invest in mining. A former small business owner, aged 50, said, “I am completely destitute… I borrowed money from many people to do business, but the stone is currently stuck.” Others could not find other work. A former mining stakeholder, aged 43, said, “We eat one meal and can’t afford to eat the other two. Thousands of people like me live like this.”","700":"The Bangladesh Environmental Lawyers Association (BELA) opposed mining as environmentally unsustainable. “Gravel mining here was never sustainable,” a BELA representative stated, pointing to erosion, sedimentation and hidden casualties among workers; pit collapses and deaths of workers had allegedly been concealed. “The community only sees the economic benefits but is blind to the dangers,” BELA asserted, citing the surge in extraction immediately before the ban (Figure 5.7) as an example of short-term profit taking precedence over sustainability. BELA argued that alternatives such as eco-tourism and small-scale agriculture would offer long-term stability: “It’s not that these people don’t deserve jobs, but we need sustainable jobs that don’t destroy their land.”","725":"Fourth, the ban on mining on the Lubha River did not arise from routine regulation, but followed a legal challenge by an environmental organization, and was preceded by a surge of extraction as operators anticipated the ban (Figure 5.7). How extraction is governed, and why it persists where it is not banned, is the subject of Chapter 8. The durability of the recovery documented herein also depends on governance, because large-scale extraction resumed at gravel extraction sites in Sylhet after the change of government in August 2024 (Section 2.4.4). Imagery after 2024 was not examined at Lubhachorra, and whether extraction resumed there is not known.","727":"Gravel mining at Lubhachorra began, peaked and stopped within approximately a decade. Its geomorphic and socio-economic transformations divide into four periods, namely pre-extraction (1972 to 2012), active-channel extraction dominance (2012 to 2015), accelerated extraction from the abandoned channels/floodplain (2016 to 2021) and recovery after the ban (2021 to 2024). On average, extraction exceeded the natural supply of sediment by c. 19% in the active channel and 61% in the abandoned channels/floodplain. Mining brought income, migrant workers and new roads, whereas the ban brought unemployment, debt and economic decline to communities that had come to depend on it. After the ban, the active channel recovered geomorphically within c. four years, whereas the floodplain pits, c. 23% infilled by 2024, still persist.","777":"Before mining (1988 to 2011), exposed bar area was larger when dry-season stage at WLd was lower, giving a negative relation between stage and bar area (slope −0.68 km²/m, r = −0.94; Figure 6.5a, b). The relation kept the same slope in the early mining period (2012 to 2016), although bar area began to fall below the pre-mining regression (Figure 6.5b, c). In the mechanized period (2017 to 2025), the relation became positive (slope +0.70 km²/m, r = +0.92; Figure 6.5b): bar area declined as dry-season stage also fell.","778":"Because falling stage would normally expose more bar, not less, this inversion marks a departure from the pre-mining relation between stage and area. The signed bar-area anomaly (Figure 6.5c), observed minus expected bar area from the pre-mining regression, was near zero before 2012 and then became increasingly negative. It averaged c. −0.30 km² in the early mining period and c. −1.88 km² in the mechanized period, and reached c. −2.8 km² in 2025.","782":"At the upstream section XS1 (Figure 6.6a, e), which the imagery shows was not directly affected by extraction until c. 2021, the bed aggraded between 2008 and 2023: the thalweg rose by c. 2.3 m and deposition extended across much of the section. The three mined sections were instead lowered and reshaped. At XS2 the thalweg lowered by c. 0.4 m, while the channel widened and parts of the bed were excavated into pits (Figure 6.6b, e). At XS3, beside the downstream gauge, the bed incised across a wide swath and the thalweg lowered by c. 10 m between 2010 and 2024 (Figure 6.6c, e). At XS4, c. 5 km farther downstream, part of the bed incised and the thalweg lowered by c. 1.7 m (Figure 6.6d, e).","787":"At the downstream gauge WLd, dry-season stage stood at c. 9.9 m through the pre-mining period and declined through the 2010s to 7.1 m by 2025, a fall of c. 2.8 m (Figure 6.7a, b). At the upstream gauge WLu, dry-season stage was constant at c. 12.3 m until 2019 and then declined to 10.6 m by 2023, 7.9 m in 2024 and 8.8 m in 2025 (Figure 6.7a, b). The fall therefore appeared first at the downstream gauge and reached the upstream gauge several years later, with the sharpest upstream fall between 2023 and 2024.","793":"At the near-river well GWn, where water-table depth is recorded weekly, larger depths indicate a lower water table (Figure 6.8a). Before mining, the dry-season water table stood at c. 3.5 m below ground with no trend (+0.05 m decade⁻¹, p = 0.73). After 2013, depth increased steadily to 5.5 m by 2024, at +1.70 m decade⁻¹ (p < 0.001; Figure 6.8a). The monsoon water table shows the same break, its depth increasing from c. 2.3 m to 4.2 m, so that the whole annual envelope of the water table moved downward, in the monsoon as in the dry season.","798":"Overbank flooding declined over the record. Days per monsoon with stage at or above 12 m at WLd fell from c. 10 before mining to c. 5 in the early mining period and 1 to 2 in the mechanized period, and days at or above 13 m fell from c. 2 to near zero (Figure 6.9b).","800":"The Sentinel-1 record does not reach back before mining, but it maps the spatial change in inundation (Figure 6.9c, d). On two monsoon dates with upstream discharge within 4% of each other, the inundated floodplain area fell from 27.96 km² on 12 August 2017, at the start of the Sentinel-1 record and of the mechanized period, to 3.45 km² on 23 September 2025, c. 88% less inundation at a comparable discharge. Because discharge is controlled in the gauge comparison and closely matched in the radar comparison, the reduced inundation is consistent with a change in the channel rather than a reduction in flow.","805":"Annual rainfall at RF1 shows no trend within either period (Mann-Kendall p = 0.19 before mining and 0.44 during it; Figure 6.10a). Mean annual rainfall during mining was c. 15% (c. 510 mm) lower than before, but under the pre-mining relation between rainfall and stage, a difference of this size corresponds to only c. 0.14 m of water level, an order of magnitude less than the observed fall (Figure 6.10a, b).","811":"Annual mean discharge at WLu shows no trend over the record (Mann-Kendall p = 0.20; least-squares slope −3.4 m³ s⁻¹ year⁻¹, not significant; Figure 6.11a), and thus reduced inflow alone does not account for the fall in stage.","812":"The stage-discharge rating has, however, shifted. At both gauges the rating during mining lies below the pre-mining rating, so that a given discharge now passes at a lower stage (Figure 6.11b), consistent with a lowered bed or an enlarged channel. The size of the shift differs between the gauges (Figure 6.11c). At WLd, the fall in stage at a fixed discharge is large and decreases with flow, from c. 2.2 m at 20 m³ s⁻¹ to 1.6 m at 1,000 m³ s⁻¹, the pattern of a locally incised and enlarged channel. At WLu, the fall is smaller and nearly uniform with discharge, at c. 1.5 m. A shift that is uniform across flows indicates that the bed at the gauge has lowered while the shape of the channel has changed little, the pattern expected of incision arriving from downstream rather than of excavation at the site (Section 3.4.2).","817":"A remaining alternative is regional forcing, such as change in rainfall-driven recharge or basin-wide groundwater abstraction. The near-river well GWn and the reference well GWref, c. 12 km west of the reach and outside it, share the same climatic and regional setting, and before mining their annual water tables rose and fell together (r = 0.59, 1988 to 2011; Figure 6.12a). GWref therefore indexes the regional recharge and abstraction signal also experienced at GWn, without the same direct river influence. The water table at GWref did not deepen during mining but became shallower after 2013, by 0.5 m decade⁻¹ (p = 0.03), whereas the water table at GWn deepened steadily (Figure 6.12a).","818":"Referenced to their pre-mining relation, GWn lies c. 0.8 m deeper than predicted from GWref during mining, and c. 1.2 m deeper in the mechanized period (Figure 6.12b). A regional cause, whether climate or abstraction, would move both wells in the same direction. Instead, the near-river well deepens beyond the shared signal. With rainfall stationary (Section 6.4.7), neither climatic variability nor regional depletion accounts for the decline at the near-river well, which includes an excess component consistent with the changes in river stage and channel form.","934":"Gauge levels examined against the cross-section surveys. At Jaflong Spill, the fall in the stage at the reference discharge at the gauge and the fall in the stage at equal low-flow area at section RMPYN2 agree in size and timing to 2020, but they differ after 2020 (Figure 7.4i). The gauge level fell by a further 1.65 m to 2023, while the bed level at RMPYN2 did not change. The dry-season level at the gauge, the 10th percentile of daily stage in January to March, was 3.0 m in 2023 and 2.8 m in 2024, below the surveyed thalweg of RMPYN2 (4.2 m). This is interpreted to mean that the channel at the gauge was more than 1 m deeper than at the section 70 m away. At Sharighat, the stage at equal low-flow area at section RMSRG3 fell by 1.7 m over 2018 to 2022, compared with a fall of 0.8 m in the stage at the reference discharge at the gauge (Figure 7.4k). At Ratnar bhanga, the section RMPYN8 did not change while the gauge level fell by 1.0 m (Figure 7.4j). The survey thus does not confirm the fall in stage at this gauge. On the Jadukata River, the level at the gauge on the fan fell by only 0.4 m over the survey decade of 2012 to 2021, and its large falls came before and after that decade (Figure 7.4l).","937":"Test 1, the fall in stage at low flow compared with high flow. A lower bed lowers the stage most at low flow (Section 7.3.4). At the four gauges on the rivers where extraction continues, the fall in stage between the first decade of record and 2021 to 2026 was larger at low flow than at high flow (Figure 7A.1a; Table 7.3). The stage fell by 3.9 m at low flow and by 2.1 m at high flow at Laurergarh, by 3.7 and 0.7 m at Jaflong Spill, by 1.4 and 0.0 m at Ratnar bhanga, and by 1.6 and 0.3 m at Sharighat. The 95% confidence interval of the difference between the falls at low and at high flow excludes zero at each of these gauges. At Lubhachorra, the stage changed by +0.1 m at low flow and by −0.1 m at high flow, and the confidence intervals of the change in stage include zero at every percentile of discharge except the 50th, where the unresolved rise in fitted stage described above occurs.","945":"The regional wells did not establish a response of groundwater level to the fall in river stage (Figure 7A.2b). The deepest dry-season depth to groundwater increased by 0.25 m decade⁻¹ at Jaintiapur and by 0.37 m decade⁻¹ at the Kanaighat reference well (both p < 0.001), with the difference between the two trends not being significant. The Tahirpur record is erratic and ends in 2021. Month-to-month changes in depth to groundwater correlate only weakly with those in river level, with Spearman r = 0.16 at Jaintiapur and 0.38 at Kanaighat. The decline in groundwater is thus regional, and it cannot be counted as evidence of bed lowering. The distance of the regional wells from the gauges, and the failed records of the wells near the rivers (Appendix 7A.4), thus leave the groundwater link untested for these four rivers.","947":"At a matched discharge, the median inundated area in 2022 to 2025 was 39% lower than in 2015 to 2018 on the Jadukata River and 32% lower on the Piyain River, but 26% higher on the Sari-Gowain River and 7% higher on the Lubha River (Figure 7A.8). None of these changes differed significantly from zero change, with 95% confidence intervals of −58 to +87%, −58 to +42%, −69 to +245% and −69 to +532%, respectively. Alternative backscatter thresholds and a regression on all images preserved the directions of change but did not establish a change (Appendix 7A.6).","950":"Across all monsoon images of the two years, at the same discharge and the same 15-day rainfall, the inundated area of the floodplain zone was smaller in 2025 than in 2017 on all three rivers where extraction continues (Figure 7.9i). Inundated area was 62% smaller on the Piyain River (95% confidence interval −76 to −42%), 35% smaller on the Jadukata River (−57 to −1%) and 27% smaller on the Sari-Gowain River (−62 to +38%). The confidence interval for the Sari-Gowain River includes zero, and thus no change can be established on that river alone. When the images of the three rivers were combined in one regression (Section 7.3.5), the inundated area in 2025 was 50% smaller than in 2017 (−63 to −32%). In the short-reach zones, the decrease in inundated area was confined to the Piyain River (−55%, 95% confidence interval −70 to −32%). The Jadukata River (−5%, −36 to +42%) and the Sari-Gowain River (+31%, −48 to +231%) showed no change, and the change of −26% for the three rivers combined has a confidence interval that includes zero (−49 to +6%). On the Lubha River, the inundated area did not change between the two years in either zone (+5% and +4%). This comparison relies on the images of only two monsoons, those of 2017 and 2025, and thus cannot show whether the difference between these two years is part of a sustained change. The 15-day rainfall was also added as a term to the comparison of the two four-year periods described at the start of this section, and it changed the results of that comparison by only a few percent.","951":"Lubha River after the ban. The Lubha River differs from the other three rivers because extraction ended there in February 2021 and the pits in the active channel refilled within four years (Chapter 5; Chyon et al., 2026). At the same discharge and 15-day rainfall, the inundated area in the monsoons of 2021 to 2025 was 81% larger than in 2015 to 2020 in the floodplain zone (−5 to +245%) and 85% larger in the short-reach zone (−12 to +256%) (Figure 7.9j). Both confidence intervals narrowly include zero change. Over the same years, the inundated area of the floodplain zone was 17% smaller on the Jadukata River (−31 to +5%) and 24% smaller on the Piyain River (−43 to +7%). The Sari-Gowain River showed an increase of 17% in the floodplain zone and of 48% in the short-reach zone (+4 to +108%) although extraction continues there, and thus an increase alone does not show recovery. The stage at the reference discharge at Lubhachorra was lowest in 2021 and 2022 and rose afterward, although not significantly (Section 7.4.6). Residents surveyed for Chapter 5 reported more flooding after the ban (survey of Chyon et al., 2026; this response is not reported in the article). The increase in inundated area on the Lubha River is thus consistent with the refilling of the channel, but it is not statistically established.","961":"Lubha River. Extraction began c. 2012, became mechanized c. 2016, peaked in 2020 to 2021 and ended with the ban of February 2021, after which the active channel refilled within c. four years (Chapter 5). Over the whole record, the gauge level shows no lasting fall, and the areas of bar and water in the channel belt show no lasting departure from their baseline (Sections 7.4.1 and 7.4.3). Within the record, however, the stage at the reference discharge was lower at the peak of extraction and directly after the ban than in the years before and after (Figure 7.10a, b). At a discharge of 6.9 m³ s⁻¹, the stage averaged 4.94 m in the dry seasons of 2016 to 2020, 4.52 m in 2021 and 2022, and 4.81 m in 2023 to 2026. The two years at the peak of extraction, and directly after the ban, were thus 0.42 m lower than the five years before them, and they are the two years of lowest stage at this discharge since 2012. The stage then rose, to 4.69 and 4.66 m in 2023 and 2024 and to 5.03 and 4.85 m in 2025 and 2026, which is similar to the level of 2016 to 2020.","962":"Three other records at Lubhachorra changed in the same direction. The low-season gauge level fell to 4.13 m in 2022, the lowest value of 2009 to 2026. This level rose to 4.49 m in 2025 and 4.57 m in 2026, although the median dry-season discharge of 2025 (3.8 m³ s⁻¹) was lower than that of 2022 (5.0 m³ s⁻¹) (Figure 7.10c). In the Landsat images taken at stages of 4.3 to 4.9 m, the channel belt had 1.17 km² of bar and 1.10 km² of water in 2016 to 2020, 0.75 and 1.31 km² in 2021 to 2022, and 0.96 and 1.11 km² in 2023 to 2026 (Figure 7.10d). Bars were thus replaced by water at the peak of extraction, with part of this bar area reforming after extraction ceased. The monsoon inundation of the floodplain was c. 80% larger after the ban than before it (Section 7.4.5).","988":"The first two links of the Someshwari impact chain, namely bed lowering and a lower river stage at the same discharge, recur most clearly on the Jadukata, Piyain and Sari-Gowain rivers. Stage at a common discharge fell by 1.0 to 3.9 m at the four gauges with discharge records on these rivers. The larger fall in stage at low flow, its build-up over years and its persistence after accounting for rainfall and discharge support a changing channel control as a result of sediment extraction. Surveys confirm bed lowering at Jaflong Spill and Sharighat, while imagery records the replacement of bars by water before the first bed surveys. Attribution of the causes of change remains weaker at Ratnar bhanga, where the bed survey does not confirm lowering and c. 50% of the decrease in stage occurred across the unrecorded year 2019.","989":"The Lubha River, where extraction ended with the ban of February 2021, shows no evidence of lasting bed lowering. Its stage at the reference discharge was 0.4 m lower in 2021 and 2022, at the peak of extraction and directly after the ban, than in 2016 to 2020, and stage had returned to its earlier level by 2025 to 2026. The low-season level, bar area detected in the Landsat images and the monsoon inundation changed in the same direction. This is a trajectory expected where the channel was lowered and then refilled (Chapter 5), although two years of low stage do not establish it. The high supply of gravel, which infilled the pits in the active channel during each monsoon, may explain why the response of this river was small.","990":"The third link, groundwater, could not be tested adequately. The regional wells, which lie 10 to 21 km from the rivers, show a regional decline in groundwater level in which no response to the rivers can be isolated, and the wells near the rivers have no reliable record. The fourth link, floodplain inundation, is supported where extraction continues. At the same discharge and the same 15-day rainfall, Sentinel-1 radar shows 50% less floodplain inundation in 2025 than in 2017 on the three rivers where extraction continues, with the decrease clearest on the Piyain River (62% reduction in inundation). On the Lubha River, inundation did not change between these two years, and was c. 80% larger after the ban of February 2021 than before it, an increase that is suggestive but not distinguishable from zero. The floodplain at Lubhachorra stands 6 to 8 m above the channel thalweg, and thus only a small response in inundation would be expected from the small change in stage recorded there.","1046":"The distribution of revenue was more concentrated. KII accounts placed leaseholders and machine owners at the top of the extraction hierarchy, followed by transport operators, managers, and laborers. In the leaseholder’s constant-sum exercise, 54 of every 100 BDT (1 USD ≈ 110 BDT during the January 2024 fieldwork) paid by a buyer went to the leaseholder syndicate and machine owner, compared with 11 BDT to laborers at the ghat. Unofficial payments accounted for 6 BDT, exceeding the 4 BDT allocated to official royalties (Figure 8.4; Table 8A.4). The exercise provides the most direct measure of benefit concentration in the present study. At the bottom of that allocation, the labor sardar reported daily wages of c. 400 to 700 BDT, with advances that tied laborers to the ghat until the debt was cleared.","1049":"Employment trends showed the same occupational divide. Compared with five years earlier, 82% of Core SM and 73% of Linked-to-SM respondents reported more jobs, whereas only 12% of Non-Related respondents shared this view; 65% of that group reported fewer jobs (Table 8.3; Figure 8.5). This was the largest group difference in the dataset (V = 0.66). Within the sample, the trajectory recalled by respondents was employment expansion inside the sand economy and decline outside it.","1050":"These gains were also gendered. The mechanized core was described as overwhelmingly male, with women concentrated in lower-paid activities such as coal cleaning, stone work, shops, and household provisioning. In the women’s FGDs, participants reported daily wages of c. 350 to 400 BDT compared with 650 to 700 for men. Given the strongly male household sample, these reports establish a pattern rather than population prevalence. Participants also linked declining female employment to the loss of agricultural land, a cost examined next.","1052":"The costs of extraction were concentrated among households whose livelihoods remained tied to land and river resources. Hampered livelihoods were reported by 74% of Core SM, 69% of Linked-to-SM, and 93% of Non-Related respondents (Table 8.2; Figure 8A.1). Frequently affected activities included agriculture, fishing, and coal and stone collection. FGD participants also emphasized riverbank erosion, land loss, declining water quality and fisheries, dust, injury risk, and damage to village roads.","1053":"Agricultural impacts showed the clearest positional difference, being reported by 71% of Non-Related respondents, compared with 46% of Core SM and 29% of Linked-to-SM respondents. Severe or very severe damage was also highest among the Non-Related group. The lower values among Linked-to-SM respondents were consistent with their greater concentration in transport and trading occupations rather than land-based livelihoods.","1058":"Land loss also had a gendered tenure dimension among Garo households. All eight participants in the Garo women’s FGD reported land held through the female line, six reported such land being used for sand-related activities without consent, and no Bengali participant reported matrilineal tenure. As a Garo woman landholder described it, “In Garo society our land comes to us through our mothers’ line. But riverbank erosion has swallowed that ancestral homestead and paddy land.” These qualitative accounts identify a specific connection between riverbank erosion and assets held by women in Garo households, but do not support prevalence estimates.","1068":"Complaint behavior illustrated what happened when dissatisfaction entered the formal system. Formal complaints were attempted by 10% of Core SM, 17% of Linked-to-SM, and 47% of Non-Related respondents. Across all three groups, 29% of classified respondents (97 of 337) reported attempting a complaint. Of those attempts, 5% (5) were reported as addressed and 43% (42) met pressure to desist. Three-quarters of those reporting pressure were Non-Related respondents (Figure 8.6b; Table 8A.4).","1073":"The persistence of sand mining could not be explained readily by lack of recognition. A majority in every occupational group judged mining-related change unsustainable: 72% of Core SM, 77% of Linked-to-SM, and 90% of Non-Related respondents (Table 8.2). This recognition therefore extended even to those receiving the most direct economic benefits.","1085":"Following the data-layered approach of Blair et al. (2021), Table 8.4 provides the evidence layer by identifying the polarity and empirical basis of each retained link. Twenty-one links were supported by triangulated survey, qualitative, and documentary evidence. One additional link, from benefit concentration to control over extraction assets, was retained as an inference because asset ownership was concentrated, but no informant described profits being reinvested directly in machinery. In this chapter, the evidence for the link from sand extraction to environmental degradation also includes the physical changes measured on the same reach in Chapter 6.","1090":"The first reinforcing loop, R1, was the livelihood loop. Sand extraction created employment and income within the sand economy. Those benefits, in turn, supported continued participation in extraction and its associated trades. Dependence deepened through advance debt and reduced ability to leave the trade, creating path dependency that further sustained extraction (Sections 8.4.1 and 8.4.2).","1092":"The second reinforcing loop, R2, operated through accumulation and political protection. Extraction produced concentrated benefits that were associated with political protection through the unofficial payments and pre-tender arrangements (Sections 8.4.1 and 8.4.3), while protection helped secure effective leases and site access. Continued access then enabled further extraction. Control over dredging machinery provided an additional direct route to extraction. The inferred link from benefit concentration to asset control would strengthen this reinforcing structure, but was not required for the core R2 loop.","1093":"Against these reinforcing processes ran loop B1, the corrective pathway. Extraction increased environmental degradation, but with a delay. Degradation fed community conflict, while also reducing land- and river-based employment and income along a parallel cost pathway. The harm-to-conflict link was supported by the concentration of conflict among cost-bearing respondents, by women’s FGD accounts linking mining-related degradation to social disruption, and by the petition triggered by loss of cropland and a village road (Sections 8.4.2 and 8.4.4). Conflict could then enter the formal system through complaints. Grievance response should then lead to enforcement, and enforcement should reduce extraction. Because the closed chain contains one negative link, loop B1 is balancing (Table 8.4). If sufficiently strong, this corrective pathway should have translated accumulating costs into reduced extraction.","1094":"The evidence indicates that loop B1 weakened near its end. Complaints were rarely resolved (c. 5% of 97 attempts), while enforcement actions produced only short interruptions before extraction resumed. Political protection entered the diagram at precisely these points, reducing both grievance response and enforcement. The same structure that strengthened loop R2 therefore weakened the pathway that could otherwise constrain extraction.","1114":"Potential remedies divided the same way between respondent groups. Banning mechanized dredgers was the modal remedy in the Non-Related group (68%) but attracted no support in either mining-linked group. Core SM respondents favored manual-only extraction (52%) and rotation of extraction sites (28%), while the Linked-to-SM group favored infrastructure development (43%) and stricter enforcement of limits (37%). Stricter enforcement was the only remedy attracting support in all three groups (16 to 37%), and runs through precisely the links that the evidence showed were suppressed. In the FGD remedy exercise, neither men’s group allocated any weight to changing leasing arrangements (Table 8A.6). Interventions aimed mainly at recognition or information would not address the binding constraint, since recognition was already high and grievance already exercised. The relevant leverage lies in the pair of protection links, and the corresponding measures are those that insulate complaint handling and enforcement from operator influence: complaint channels outside the local protection network, and transparent tender and machinery records. Although the CLD locates this constraint, it does not supply the political conditions for acting on it.","1116":"The limitations shared with other chapters, including the non-probability sample and the reliance on recall, are set out in Sections 3.4.4 and 3.8, and those specific to this chapter are discussed below. Contrasts in responses between groups hold within the sample recruited near active ghats. The design is cross-sectional, with causal direction taken from triangulated mechanism evidence rather than association (Section 8.3.3). The concentration estimates (c. 900 dredging machines under 14 operators, with one controlling more than half) come from a single informant. They are consistent with interviews, group discussions, and documentary reporting, but were not audited independently. With 98% of respondents male, the gender material establishes presence, but not prevalence. Furthermore, the case concerns one reach under one dominant operator, and hence it is the evidence-linked analytical procedure and link structure that are transferable, rather than the magnitudes. The study boundary also excludes the district- and national-level political relations behind lease allocation and protection. Their effects on the reach are evidenced, but their own dynamics were not traced (Section 8.6.2). Loop dominance was assessed from convergent observations rather than estimated, and the CLD does not predict responses to intervention (Section 8.2.2). Finally, the analysis is bounded temporally by the February 2024 High Court stay (Section 8.3.1), and thus whether the same structure re-forms under subsequent arrangements, including the continued unauthorized extraction reported since leasing was suspended (Section 2.5.1), is an open question that the same instruments can answer.","1144":"Hypothesis 1 addressed the first research question, which asked how sediment extraction in northern Bangladesh grew after the late 1980s and how that growth related to national economic growth and policy (RQ1; Section 1.9). Chapter 4 answered this by following 337 fixed sites through January Landsat images from 1988 to 2025 (Section 4.3.1). Extraction proved to be largely recent. It first became visible from 2000 onward at 72 of the 77 sites that could be dated, and its footprint, the area of former sand converted to water or pits, grew c. fourfold, from c. 260 ha in 2000 to c. 1,000 ha in 2023 (Figure 9.1a, b). This growth followed the long-term rise in national demand for construction materials, measured by cement output (Spearman’s rho = 0.97). The first part of the hypothesis, concerning economic growth, is therefore consistent with the timing of extraction, although the thesis could not trace where the sand and gravel were used (Section 4.4.1).","1145":"The second part, regarding policy, predicted that bans would move extraction rather than reduce it, and this is supported (Section 4.4.1). After a court order in 2014, and the declaration of Jaflong as an ecologically critical area in 2015, restricted the sites of gravel mining, extraction grew on the sand-bed rivers, where the footprint rose from c. 240 to c. 440 ha by 2020. Imports of gravel from India and Bhutan rose over the same years from c. 0.5 to 7.5 Mt year⁻¹ (Section 4.3.2). When a national ban on gravel extraction was enacted in February 2020, the regional footprint still did not fall, and it stood at c. 1,000 ha in 2023 (Figure 9.1b). Bans thus changed where extraction took place and where the material came from, but not the regional extent of extraction. National decisions also set its timing, because extraction at Jaflong and Bholaganj resumed within days of the change of government in August 2024.","1148":"Hypothesis 2 addressed the second research question, which asked how gravel extraction on the Lubha River compared with the supply of sediment, and how the river and its community responded to extraction and to its end (RQ2; Section 1.9). The first part of this hypothesis predicted that the active channel, which receives sediment in every monsoon, would recover before the abandoned channels and the floodplain, which receive sediment only during high floods. This part is supported, and from 2012 to 2021, extraction exceeded the natural supply of gravel by c. 19% in the active channel and by 61% in the abandoned channels and the floodplain (Section 5.4.5). After the ban of February 2021, the active channel refilled within c. four years, leaving only 5.5% of its mined area unfilled by 2024, whereas the pits in the abandoned channels and the floodplain were only 23% infilled (Figure 9.3a).","1149":"The second part of the hypothesis predicted that livelihoods would not recover with the geomorphology of the river, and this is also supported. Three years after the ban on mining, 95% of the 55 residents surveyed at Lubhachorra reported hardship and unemployment, and 73% had petitioned the authorities to reopen extraction (Figure 9.3b; Table 5.2). The river recovered, but the income that extraction had provided did not return.","1152":"Hypothesis 3 addressed the third research question, which asked whether mechanized extraction had changed the Someshwari River and its floodplain, whether these changes could be attributed to extraction although its volumes are unrecorded, and whether four further rivers showed the same changes (RQ3; Section 1.9). The hypothesis predicted a chain of four linked changes. Extraction lowers the riverbed, the stage at the same discharge then falls, mostly at low flow, the water table beside the river deepens, and the floodplain becomes inundated less often. On the Someshwari River, all four links were established after rainfall, discharge and regional groundwater had been accounted for (Figure 9.1c; Sections 6.4.7 to 6.4.9). The bed was lowered by up to c. 10 m at one cross-section, the stage at the same low discharge fell by 2.2 m, the water table deepened by c. 0.17 m year⁻¹ after 2013, and the inundated area of the floodplain was 88% smaller in 2025 than in 2017, although this last result relies on one pair of images.","1153":"Chapter 7 tested whether this impact chain recurs on the Jadukata, Piyain, Sari-Gowain and Lubha rivers, using the routine records of the Bangladesh Water Development Board (Table 9.1; Figure 9.2). On the three rivers still being mined, the first two links recur. The stage at the same low discharge fell by 1.4 to 3.9 m, and on the Piyain and Sari-Gowain rivers it fell most at the gauges nearer the extraction sites (Section 7.5.1). Repeated cross-sectional surveys confirmed that the bed had been lowered on all three rivers, although not at every gauge (Section 7.4.2). A decrease in floodplain inundation was established on the Piyain River, where the inundated area was 62% smaller in 2025 than in 2017, and indicated on the Jadukata River, where it was 35% smaller (Section 7.4.5). The response of the water table could not be evaluated on these rivers, because no well near the sites of excavation had a reliable record (Section 7.4.4).","1154":"The Lubha River, where extraction ended with the ban of February 2021, provides a contrast. Its stage at the same discharge was 0.4 m lower in 2021 and 2022, at the peak of extraction, but this fall was not statistically significant and had disappeared by 2025 to 2026, as the pits in its active channel refilled (Sections 7.4.6 and 9.2.2). Its floodplain, 6 to 8 m above the bed, was too high to be affected by so small a change (Section 7.5.2). The first two links therefore persist where extraction continues, whereas on the Lubha River the stage recovered once extraction stopped.","1157":"Hypothesis 4 addressed the fourth research question, which asked how far households along the Someshwari River recognized the harm of extraction, and why extraction persisted (RQ4; Section 1.9). The hypothesis predicted that extraction persists because the feedbacks that sustain it weaken the feedbacks that would restrain it, and this is supported by the evidence. Recognition of the harm of extraction was high, with 72 to 90% of respondents in every occupational group judging the changes to the river unsustainable (Section 8.4.5). Persistence therefore cannot be explained by a failure to recognize the harm.","1158":"However, recognition did not lead to restraint, because the path from complaint to enforcement was weakened. Of the 97 respondents who attempted a formal complaint, only five reported that it was addressed, and 42 met pressure to desist (Figure 9.1d; Section 8.4.4). The revenue of extraction was concentrated among a few individuals, and this paid for protection. The leaseholder interviewed allocated 54 of every 100 Bangladeshi taka paid by a buyer to the leaseholder syndicate and the machine owner, and only 11 to laborers, with unofficial payments exceeding the official royalty (Sections 8.4.1 and 8.4.3). Chapter 8 termed this structure an impunity trap. In this trap, the dependence of households on extraction, and the protection bought by its revenue, sustain extraction, while protection weakens the two links, the response to complaints and enforcement, through which the recognition of harm could restrain extraction (Section 8.5.2).","1165":"Bed material alone does not explain how the rivers responded, because the three gravel-bed rivers, the Lubha, Piyain and Dhalai, did not respond alike (Figure 9.2). The Lubha River refilled its active channel within c. four years of its ban, whereas the stage of the Piyain River at the Jaflong Spill gauge fell by 3.7 m, and the pits at Bholaganj on the Dhalai River held pools of water throughout the national ban of 2020 to 2024 (Table 7.3; Section 4.3.6).","1166":"The comparison indicates that what mattered was the depth of excavation relative to the supply of sediment. On the Lubha River, the pits in the active channel were shallow and lay in the path of the gravel delivered by each monsoon (Section 5.3.1). At Jaflong and Bholaganj, the bed was excavated below the low-flow water surface, and the bars that could have refilled the pits were removed (Sections 4.3.1 and 7.4.1). Kondolf (1994) drew the same distinction between skimming bars, which a river can replenish, and digging pits below the water surface, which trap incoming sediment and lower the bed upstream and downstream.","1169":"The method of extraction determined the depth of excavation. On the Lubha River, hand extraction from 2012 left pits of c. 3 m in the active channel, whereas excavators, used from 2016 in the abandoned channels and the floodplain, dug pits of c. 5 m that have subsequently recovered least (Sections 5.3.1 and 5.4.3). On the Someshwari River, the dry-season stage fell fastest after mechanized dredging began in 2017 (Figure 9.3d). Large changes did not require dredgers, however. On the Jadukata and Sari-Gowain rivers, mined mainly from boats and with pumps over many years, the stage at the same low discharge fell by 3.9 m and 1.6 m, against 2.2 m on the dredged Someshwari River (Section 2.4.2; Table 7.3).","1175":"At Jaflong, the ban was not followed by recovery. The dry-season stage at the Jaflong Spill gauge kept falling after February 2020, faster than before (Figure 9.3c). Chapter 7 could not assess whether extraction continued downstream of the leased site, or whether the channel was still adjusting to a pit dug below the low-flow water surface, which draws in the low flow and grows upstream (Section 7.5.2; Kondolf, 1997). On the Someshwari River, where extraction did not stop, the stage also kept falling (Figure 9.3d).","1178":"Section 1.2.2 proposed that sand and gravel mining is difficult to manage because the four stages of the DMP-SGM pathway operate at different scales. Figure 9.4a tests this by placing each finding by the distance and the time over which it operated. Demand and the growth of extraction were national and lasted decades, and the lowering of the rivers extended along reaches of up to c. 12 km over a decade or more. However, the decisions that permitted extraction were far smaller. A lease covered one block of a river for one year, and a complaint concerned one site (Sections 2.5.1 and 8.4.4). Only the ban on the Lubha River and the national ban of 2020 to 2024 lasted for several years, and both came from outside the lease system (points 11 and 13 in Figure 9.4a).","1183":"These two problems reinforce each other. A one-year lease gives its holder no stake in the future condition of the river, and protection removes the remaining restraint: the complaints of those who bear the costs. The end of the national ban within days of the change of government in August 2024 is consistent with this, because the ban depended on national authority rather than on the leases. Monitoring at the scale of the river is therefore necessary, but it will always be insufficient unless complaints and enforcement are protected from the influence of operators.","1188":"The first contribution is a site-based reconstruction of sediment extraction from an archive of satellite images. Chapter 4 first tested a machine-learning classification of sand, water and vegetation along the whole corridor, which detected extraction only where a large, persistent bare surface was left. Because its composite images combined scenes taken at different river stages, the areas of sand and water detected could not be compared between years (Section 4.2.1). Chapter 4 therefore examined 337 fixed sites in single January images taken at low flow, and assigned each site to a class of change (Section 4.2). This method dates the onset of visible change at each site and yields a conservative footprint of extraction, although it cannot measure volumes and detects extraction below the water surface only after the channel has changed form. On the Someshwari River, for example, Landsat images first detected change in 2021, whereas extraction began in c. 2012 (Section 4.4.2).","1189":"The second contribution is a sequence of tests that attribute hydrological change to sediment extraction using routine monitoring records (Section 3.4.2; Table 3.4). The sequence compares river stage at a fixed discharge before and after extraction began, and it then tests whether rainfall, discharge, regional groundwater or a fall in water level transmitted from downstream could explain the change. Chapter 6 developed the sequence on the Someshwari River, and Chapter 7 applied the method to four additional rivers using existing records collected by the Bangladesh Water Development Board. Because the sequence requires no record of the volume extracted, it suits the many rivers where no such record exists (Koehnken et al., 2020).","1190":"The third contribution is the combination of a household survey with a causal loop diagram in which each link is tied to its evidence. Of the 22 links in the diagram presented in Chapter 8, 21 were supported by survey, interview and documentary evidence together, and one was retained as an inference (Section 8.5.1). The causal loop diagram also locates the links at which the corrective pathway failed, and it thereby turns a general finding of weak governance into specific points for intervention. However, this remains a qualitative explanation and does not predict the effect of an intervention (Section 8.6.4).","1191":"The alignment of the three types of evidence by reach, by dry season and by dated event produced three outcomes (Figure 9.5). Where the evidence converged, attribution was stronger than any one record could provide, and such convergence is termed an established result in Section 9.1. On the Someshwari River, the imagery recorded a loss of exposed bar, the surveys revealed a lowered riverbed, the gauge showed a fall in river stage and the near-river well a deeper water table, all after extraction began, and residents reported bank erosion and land loss (Section 8.4.2). Where one type of evidence showed what another could not, the conclusion was widened. On the Lubha River, the images showed a channel that had refilled, and only the social survey revealed that livelihoods had not recovered. Where the evidence disagreed, the disagreement marked the limit of a method. For example, at the Ratnar bhanga gauge on the Piyain River, the stage at the same low discharge fell by 1.4 m, although the surveyed cross-section showed no lowering (Section 7.4.2). These discordant results were reported as they were found, and not resolved in favor of any attribution.","1195":"Recommendations 1 to 3 link extraction to the condition of the river. The check in recommendation 1 uses gauges and surveys that the Bangladesh Water Development Board already operates, and these records already showed the stage falling on three rivers while their leases were renewed each year (Sections 7.5.4 and 9.4). The check would be strengthened by data from a surveyed cross-section and a groundwater well near each gauge. Recommendation 2 follows from the comparison of the six rivers, which indicates that the depth of excavation relative to supply determined whether and how fast a river recovered (Section 9.3.1). A limit of this kind requires an estimate of sediment supply for each river, as was made for the Lubha River (Section 5.4.5). Recommendation 3 applies where artisanal extraction from bars can continue within that limit, because regulated extraction of this kind retains local employment at a smaller cost to the river than mechanized extraction (Section 5.6.2). Chapter 5 proposed that local management boards, composed of representatives of the community, local government and environmental organizations, should delineate extraction zones and monitor compliance. Such boards could also hold the records required by recommendations 1 and 5.","1196":"Recommendation 4 follows from the Lubha River, where the ban restored the geomorphology of the active channel within c. four years, but no measure addressed the loss of income. The physical benefit and the social cost of a ban arise from the same decision, and both should be addressed in that decision. Where the bed has been excavated below the low-flow water surface, a ban serves first to stop further extraction, and recovery, which depends on the supply of sediment from upstream, should not be expected within a few years (Section 9.3.3).","1197":"Recommendations 5 and 6 address the two links at which the corrective pathway failed (Figure 9.4b). Stricter enforcement was the only remedy supported in all three occupational groups on the Someshwari River (Section 8.6.3). Enforcement, however, operates through the links that protection weakened, and it therefore requires records that are open to scrutiny, as well as a route for complaints that cannot be blocked by the local political network. Recommendation 7 would provide the regional record of extraction sites that no agency now keeps, for which the online atlas (Chyon & Best, 2026a) and the archive of evidence in Bangladesh Sand Watch (Chyon & Best, 2026b) provide a starting point.","1202":"First, the regional record quantifies sediment mining by area and not volume and may detect extraction late in its development (Section 9.5), and the volume extracted was estimated only for the Lubha River. Repeated bathymetric surveys of the mined reaches, as on the Mekong River (Gruel et al., 2022; Hackney et al., 2021; Lau et al., 2023), would allow extraction to be compared with sediment supply on the sand-bed rivers.","1203":"Second, the attribution of hydrological change to extraction relies on five gauged rivers. All four links of the impact chain were tested on the Someshwari River, and the first two were established on three further rivers from routine monitoring records alone (Chapters 6 and 7). The tests of Chapters 6 and 7 could be applied to the other extraction sites mapped in Chapter 4 wherever gauge records exist (Section 4.2.4), and a cross-section and a near-river well at each gauge would allow all four links of the impact chain to be tested there. The same tests could also measure the effect of dredgers on larger rivers, such as the dredging of the Old Brahmaputra River for navigation since 2019 (Section 4.3.2). The Someshwari and Piyain rivers should be monitored from the enactment of any future ban through its enforcement, to show whether, and how fast, a deeply excavated bed recovers. Continued groundwater records near the Lubha River would show in the same way whether, and after how long, the aquifer recovers once extraction has stopped (Section 7.4.4).","1204":"Third, sediment was not traced from the rivers to its places of use, and the demand stage therefore relies on national indicators (Section 4.4.1). Records of transport and trade would allow Hypothesis 1 to be tested with measured sediment flows.","1208":"Sand and gravel remain essential to the development of Bangladesh, and their sustainable extraction therefore depends on two conditions being met together. River and land-use managers need limits set by the sediment budget of each river and checked against its monitoring records, so that extraction is planned within the integrated management of water, sediment and land rather than leased block by block. Complaints and enforcement need protection from the influence of operators, so that the economic benefits of extraction, its costs to farmers and the restoration of excavated land are weighed in the same decision. By dating the spread of extraction across a region, tracing its effects from the riverbed to the aquifer and the floodplain, and locating the links at which political protection holds extraction in place, the thesis joins the physical and social sides of sand and gravel mining, which have largely been studied apart. This new understanding extends to the many alluvial rivers of South Asia and beyond whose floodplains sustain farming, fishing and other river-based livelihoods, and where extraction is seldom recorded. As the demand for construction materials continues to grow, the future of these rivers, and of the communities whose livelihoods depend on them, will rely on the capacity of science and governance to keep the extraction of sediment within the limits that rivers can sustain.","799":"Mean monsoon discharge at WLu was nearly identical before and during mining (375 and 377 m³ s⁻¹), whereas mean monsoon stage at WLd was lower during mining (Figure 6.9a). Before mining, mean monsoon stage varied little with mean monsoon discharge from year to year across the observed range (r = −0.02, p = 0.94), and thus the two periods can be compared at a given discharge. At the same discharge, stage during mining was c. 1.5 m below the pre-mining level (Welch t = 6.2, p < 0.001; Figure 6.9a)."},"tables":{"7.3":[["Gauge (river)","Stage fall, low / high flow (m)","Level change, low season / monsoon (m)","Timing of fall","Channel share","Section / gauge (m)","Channel belt","Verdict"],["Laurergarh (Jadukata)","−3.9 / −2.1","−4.6 / −1.8","Gradual, 80% over 16 years, steepest 2009 to 2013","100%","No section at the gauge. RMJDR1 to 6, 9 to 21 km downstream, −1.7 to −3.3 / −0.4 (2012 to 2021)","Bars replaced by water from 2000, inverted relation 2000 to 2011","Bed lowering, strong. The gauge records the fan, and the surveys record bed lowering 9 to 21 km downstream of it"],["Jaflong Spill (Piyain, spill channel)","−3.7 / −0.7","−4.2 / −0.7","Ramp from 2017, 80% over 18 years","99%","RMPYN2 at the gauge, −2.4 / −3.5 (2012 to 2023)","Inverted relation from 2017, bar area a further  c.  33% smaller after 2021","Bed lowering, strong, confirmed by the sections and the channel belt. Gauge and section differ after 2020"],["Ratnar bhanga (Piyain, main channel)","−1.4 / 0.0","−1.5 / −0.3","c.  50% of the fall across the missing year 2019, step and ramp not separable","87%","RMPYN8 at the gauge, 0.0 / −1.0","Not analyzed","Bed lowering, moderate, not seen at the section 70 m away. The unrecorded year 2019 holds  c.  50% of the fall"],["Sharighat (Sari-Gowain)","−1.6 / −0.3","−1.8 / −0.2","Steep phase 2017 to 2021, 80% over 12 years","98%","RMSRG3 at the gauge, −1.7 / −0.8 (2018 to 2022). Pits of 4 and 6 m nearby","Bar area halved from 2008, no bar exposed by the fall in stage","Bed lowering, strong, confirmed by the sections and the channel belt. Largest at the upstream gauge"],["Gowainghat (Sari-Gowain)","No discharge","−0.9 / −0.2","Not tested","No discharge","No section","Not analyzed","Consistent with bed lowering, weaker than at Sharighat"],["Lubhachorra (Lubha)","+0.1 / −0.1","−0.5 / −0.6","No trend","No fall","No sections","No change","No lasting lowering. Stage at 6.9 m³ s⁻¹ 0.4 m lower in 2021 to 2022, returned by 2025 to 2026 (Figure 7.10). Not significant"]],"9.1":[["Hypothesis","Prediction","Outcome","Evidence"],["1, demand","Extraction begins and expands as national demand grows","Consistent in timing; no link to the destination of sediment was measured","72 of 77 dated onsets from 2000 (Section 4.4.1)"],["1, governance","Bans and suspensions shift extraction to other rivers rather than reduce its regional extent","Supported","Total area of the regional footprint of extraction did not fall during the national ban of 2020 to 2024 (Section 4.4.1)"],["2, river","The active channel recovers before abandoned channels and the floodplain","Supported","Channel pits refilled by 2024; floodplain pits 23% infilled (Sections 5.4.4 and 5.4.5)"],["2, livelihoods","Livelihoods do not recover with the river","Supported","95% of respondents reported hardship and unemployment (Table 5.2)"],["3, Someshwari River","After extraction begins, the bed lowers, stage at a given discharge falls most at low flow, the near-river water table falls more than regional groundwater, and the floodplain is inundated less often","Supported for all four links","Sections 6.4.3 to 6.4.9"],["3, Jadukata River","As above, with the largest fall at the gauges nearest the extraction sites","Bed and stage lowered, the bed 9 to 21 km downstream of the gauge; impacts on the water table could not be evaluated; decrease in inundation indicated","Sections 7.4.2 to 7.4.5"],["3, Piyain River","As above","Bed and stage lowered, not confirmed by survey at Ratnar bhanga; impacts on the water table could not be evaluated; decrease in inundation established","Sections 7.4.2 to 7.4.5"],["3, Sari-Gowain River","As above","Bed and stage lowered; impacts on the water table could not be evaluated; decrease in inundation not distinguishable from zero","Sections 7.4.2 to 7.4.5"],["3, Lubha River","As above","No lasting lowering; stage 0.4 m lower in 2021 to 2022, not significant","Sections 7.4.3 and 7.4.6"],["4","Extraction persists because the feedbacks that sustain it weaken those that would restrain it","Supported; harm was recognized","Sections 8.4.4 and 8.5.2"]],"9.2":[["Recommendation","Scale of operation","Basis"],["1. Check the stage at a fixed low discharge, and the latest surveyed cross-section, before each lease is renewed","Reach, each year","Sections 6.5.3 and 7.5.4"],["2. Limit extraction to the quantified supply of sediment, and keep excavation above the low-flow water surface","River, each year","Sections 5.4.5, 5.6.2 and 9.3.1"],["3. Rotate extraction zones along the active channel, and make the restoration of floodplain pits a condition of the lease","River, several years","Section 5.6.2"],["4. Accompany any ban with measures for supplementing the livelihoods of those who depended on extraction","Community, at the time of the ban","Sections 5.5.2 and 5.6.2"],["5. Publish leases, tenders and records of machinery","District, each year","Sections 4.3.5 and 8.6.3"],["6. Provide a complaint channel outside the local administration","District to nation","Sections 8.4.4 and 8.6.3"],["7. Maintain a regional record of extraction sites, updated each dry season","Region, each year","Sections 4.3.2 and 4.4.2"]],"8A.4Revenue":[["Recipient","BDT per 100 paid by the buyer"],["Leaseholder / block syndicate","34"],["Machine / dredger owner","20"],["Boat or truck owner and driver","15"],["Laborers at the ghat","11"],["Fuel and maintenance","10"],["Unofficial payments","6"],["Government royalty (official)","4"],["Total","100"]],"8A.4Complaints":[["Group","Never tried","Tried, no response","Tried, faced pressure","Tried, addressed","Attempted","Group size"],["Core SM","95","5","5","1","11","106"],["Linked-to-SM","62","6","6","1","13","75"],["Non-Related","83","39","31","3","73","156"],["Total","240","50","42","5","97","337"]]}};
